{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what I found and proved.\n\n## The two pointer tables (PRG ROM)\n\nBoth are read by the level-init routine at CPU `$9C22` (`prgRom 0x1C22`), which builds two 16-bit pointers per area. Each table has a small base-offset array plus parallel low/high byte arrays; a running index is `base[area_type] + $074F`.\n\n**Layout data (objects)** — `prgRom` offsets:\n| part | CPU | prgRom | size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| base offsets | `$9D28` | `0x1D28` | 4 |\n| low bytes | `$9D2C` | `0x1D2C` | 34 |\n| high bytes | `$9D4E` | `0x1D4E` | 34 |\n\n`LDY $074E / LDA $9D28,Y / CLC / ADC $074F / TAY / LDA $9D2C,Y → $E7 / LDA $9D4E,Y → $E8`. The address points at a 2-byte area header; records start at +2.\n\n**Enemy data** — `prgRom` offsets:\n| part | CPU | prgRom | size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| base offsets | `$9CE0` | `0x1CE0` | 4 |\n| low bytes | `$9CE4` | `0x1CE4` | 34 |\n| high bytes | `$9D06` | `0x1D06` | 34 |\n\n`LDA $9CE0,Y / CLC / ADC $074F / TAY / LDA $9CE4,Y → $E9 / LDA $9D06,Y → $EA`.\n\n## Proofs (in the throwaway hack `eval-sheet4-ptrs`, all undone)\n\n- **Trace**: a read trace over `0x1CE0–0x1D6F` during a 1-1 reload returned exactly the six loader reads — `$9C34`→`$9CE1`=6, `$9C3C`→`$9CEF`=1, `$9C41`→`$9D11`=`$9F` (enemy `$9F01`), and `$9C49`→`$9D29`=3, `$9C51`→`$9D34`=`$8E`, `$9C56`→`$9D56`=`$A6` (layout header `$A68E`).\n- **Layout table**: ledgered `write_memory prgRom 0x1D34` `0x8E→0xF3`, reset, re-entered 1-1 → the level rendered completely differently (tall green-topped pipe platforms instead of the ?-block/brick start), and live `$E7/$E8` became `$A6F5` (the adjacent area's header `$A6F3` + 2). `undo_edit` restored `0x8E`.\n- **Enemy table**: ledgered `write_memory prgRom 0x1CEF` `0x01→0x00`, reset, matched 175-frame run → live `$E9/$EA`=`$9F00` and `list_sprites` found **no Goomba** (only Mario + HUD coin), versus a 6-tile palette-7 Goomba at (120,185) in the unedited baseline. `undo_edit` restored `0x01`.\n\n## The data those tables point at for 1-1\n- **Objects**: `prgRom 0x2690`, 98 bytes (49 × 2-byte records; header at `0x268E`).\n- **Enemies**: `prgRom 0x1F01`, 30 bytes (3-byte records, `$FF` terminator at `0x1F1E`).\n\n## Recording\nRecorded a `level` mapping for `1-1` (both `rom_origins`) and two `note` mappings for the pointer tables (all three with their `rom_origins`, source `trace and ledgered write`), called `promote_mappings` once, and switched back to the `default` hack. Both ledgered test edits were undone, so no ROM bytes remain changed."}
